(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-anti-infectives-quiz"]={"title":"Ocular Delivery & Anti-infectives Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Amphotericin B appears on the ophthalmic antifungal table with more routes than any other agent. Which are they?","choices":["Topical solution and oral tablet only","Topical, subconjunctival, intravitreal, intravenous","Intravitreal and subconjunctival injection only","Topical suspension, and nothing else"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the one agent given by every route on the table, which is what makes it usable for endophthalmitis as well as surface disease. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which organisms are listed as infectious causes of conjunctivitis?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Serratia marcescens only","Aspergillus, Candida, Fusarium and Cephalosporium","Adenovirus, herpes simplex and varicella zoster only","Neisseria, Haemophilus, Streptococcus and Moraxella"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Though most conjunctivitis is viral, allergic, irritant or contact-lens related — the bacterial list matters because it is what empiric broad-spectrum therapy is aimed at. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which route is described for anterior segment surgery and infection, with prompt absorption and a relatively short duration?","choices":["Topical instillation","Retrobulbar injection","Oral administration","Intracameral injection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prompt onset, used in anterior segment surgery and infection, but its effect is relatively short-lived and it carries corneal and intraocular toxicity. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why do gels, ointments and solid inserts increase the amount of drug absorbed compared with a simple solution?","choices":["They prolong contact time in the cul-de-sac","They increase the drug's lipid solubility","They open tight junctions in the corneal epithelium","They reduce binding of drug to tear proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. More time sitting in the cul-de-sac means more opportunity for transcorneal diffusion. Every formulation trick is aimed at the same variable — how long the drug stays in contact with the eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 6-7"},{"q":"A patient has adenoviral conjunctivitis. Which antiviral is indicated?","choices":["Topical trifluridine","None is indicated","Topical ganciclovir","Oral acyclovir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. There is no antiviral for viral conjunctivitis caused by adenoviruses. It runs its own course and you relieve the irritation. The ocular antivirals exist for keratitis, herpes zoster ophthalmicus and retinitis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which ocular antivirals are given by mouth, and for which conditions?","choices":["Oral natamycin for fungal keratitis","Oral foscarnet for cytomegalovirus retinitis","Acyclovir, valacyclovir or famciclovir","Oral trifluridine for herpes simplex keratoconjunctivitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The systemic agents handle zoster ophthalmicus and simplex keratitis or iridocyclitis, while trifluridine and ganciclovir are the topical options. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What are the stated advantages of the topical route?","choices":["Sustained release without repeated dosing","Reliable delivery to the posterior segment","Avoidance of any systemic exposure","Convenient, economical and relatively safe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That combination is why almost every ophthalmic drug is a drop or an ointment. The price is compliance, surface toxicity and systemic absorption by the nasolacrimal route. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Sulfacetamide and trimethoprim both interfere with folate, but at different points. What is the difference?","choices":["Sulfacetamide antagonizes p-aminobenzoic acid; trimethoprim blocks reduction to tetrahydrofolate","Sulfacetamide blocks reduction to tetrahydrofolate; trimethoprim antagonizes p-aminobenzoic acid","Sulfacetamide inhibits the 30S ribosome; trimethoprim inhibits the 50S","Both block the same enzyme, so they are interchangeable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sulfacetamide acts at the synthesis end by competing with p-aminobenzoic acid; trimethoprim acts one step later, stopping folic acid being reduced. Sequential blockade of the same pathway. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 18-19"},{"q":"Two ophthalmic antibiotic classes both stop bacterial protein synthesis. What separates them?","choices":["Macrolides act at the 30S subunit, aminoglycosides at the 50S","Macrolides act at the 50S subunit, aminoglycosides at the 30S","Macrolides block transpeptidation, aminoglycosides block DNA gyrase","Both act at the 50S subunit but bind different sites"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Same endpoint, different ribosomal subunit — the cleanest mechanism pairing among the ophthalmic antibiotics. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 15 and 23"},{"q":"Which delivery route carries the risk of globe perforation, optic nerve trauma and central retinal artery occlusion?","choices":["Topical instillation","Periocular injection","Oral administration","Intravenous administration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These periocular injections reach the anterior segment, posterior uveitis and cystoid macular edema, but the needle is the hazard — perforation, optic nerve trauma, vascular occlusion and direct retinal drug toxicity if the globe is entered. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which factors determine the rate and extent of absorption of a topical ophthalmic drug?","choices":["Corneal blood flow and the rate of aqueous production","The patient's systemic renal and hepatic function","Contact time, drainage, protein binding and diffusion","Intraocular pressure and pupil size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Four variables, and formulation changes act on the first two — which is why gels, ointments and inserts exist and why occluding the puncta increases absorption. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which risk factor for ophthalmic fungal infection is one a clinician can create?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid use","Contact lens wear","Antecedent trauma","Chronic ocular surface disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The risk list is trauma, chronic ocular surface disease, contact lens wear and immunosuppression — and topical steroid use is named explicitly as immunosuppression. It is the only one on the list you can introduce by prescribing. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic must be avoided outright in a patient with a sulfonamide allergy?","choices":["Sulfacetamide","Trimethoprim with polymyxin B","Bacitracin","Ciprofloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a sulfonamide, and it works by antagonizing p-aminobenzoic acid to block folic acid synthesis. A documented sulfonamide allergy rules it out — one of the few genuine contraindications in the whole ophthalmic antibiotic list. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Beyond ocular irritation, which adverse effect is specific to trifluridine?","choices":["A white corneal precipitate","Raised intraocular pressure","Changes in iris color","Punctate keratopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is worth separating from the generic irritation every ophthalmic drug can cause, because this one names a drug and an actual corneal finding. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which agent is described as the most common ophthalmic antibiotic, and what property lets it be used before a bacterial cause is confirmed?","choices":["Azithromycin, because it is dosed less often","Ciprofloxacin, because it covers the widest spectrum","Erythromycin, because it is soothing on an inflamed eye","Bacitracin, because it has no drug interactions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is the one you will see most, and because it soothes the inflamed eye it can reasonably be started even when a bacterial infection has not been confirmed. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Bacitracin ointment works on a structure none of the other ophthalmic antibiotics target. Which one?","choices":["The 50S ribosomal subunit","DNA gyrase","The bacterial cell wall","The cytoplasmic membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bacitracin stops mucopeptides being transferred into the growing wall. Every other ophthalmic antibiotic goes after the ribosome, DNA, folate or the membrane. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does trifluridine interfere with viral replication?","choices":["It competitively inhibits deoxyguanosine triphosphate binding to DNA polymerase","It inhibits viral neuraminidase","It inhibits thymidylate synthetase and is incorporated into viral DNA in place of thymidine","It blocks viral attachment to the corneal epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It both starves the virus of thymidine and substitutes itself into the growing strand — two hits on the same pathway. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A patient on long-term chloroquine develops a bull's eye lesion in the retina. Which pharmacokinetic property explains it?","choices":["Chloroquine is metabolized in the eye to a toxic product","Nasolacrimal drainage delivers a high local dose","Chloroquine binds tear proteins and is retained on the surface","Some drugs accumulate in ocular tissue after systemic absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Distribution into the eye happens normally after systemic absorption, and a few drugs concentrate there over time. The bull's eye maculopathy is the named consequence. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is notable about natamycin among the ophthalmic antifungals?","choices":["It is the only commercially available ophthalmic antifungal","It is the only antifungal effective against Fusarium","It is the only antifungal given intravitreally","It is the only antifungal free of ocular irritation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Everything else on the antifungal table — amphotericin B, the imidazoles — is given by another route or has to be compounded. Availability is why natamycin is the one to know by name. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which non-infectious causes of conjunctivitis are commoner than the bacterial ones?","choices":["Only bacterial pathogens are listed as common","Viruses, allergy, irritants and contact lenses","Tumors and systemic disease","Fungal keratitis and herpes zoster"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are given as the common causes, with immune-mediated reactions, systemic disease and tumors as less common. Bacterial pathogens come after — a reminder that most red eyes are not bacterial. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
